illimitably invaded the Atlantic and Pacific, and though a sworn foe to human

209
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:51,558
bloodshed, yet had he in his straightbodied coat spilled tons upon

210
00:17:51,559 --> 00:17:57,317
tons of Leviathan gore. How now, in the contemplative evening of his days, the

211
00:17:57,318 --> 00:18:02,679
pious Bildad reconciled these things in his reminiscence, I do not know, but it

212
00:18:02,680 --> 00:18:07,477
did not seem to concern him much, and very probably he had long since come to

213
00:18:07,478 --> 00:18:12,199
the sage and sensible conclusion that a man's religion is one thing, and this

214
00:18:12,200 --> 00:18:17,638
practical world quite another. This world pays dividends, rising from a

215
00:18:17,639 --> 00:18:22,519
little cabin boy in short clothes of the drabus drab to a harpooner in the broad

216
00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:27,237
shadbellied waste coat. From that becoming boat header, chief mate and

217
00:18:27,238 --> 00:18:31,878
captain, and finally a ship owner, Billad, as I hinted before, had

218
00:18:31,879 --> 00:18:36,438
concluded his adventurous career by wholly retiring from active life at the

219
00:18:36,439 --> 00:18:41,798
goodly age of 60 and dedicating his remaining days to the quiet receiving of

220
00:18:41,799 --> 00:18:44,599
his well-earned income.

221
00:18:44,600 --> 00:18:49,477
Now, Bildad, I am sorry to say, had the reputation of being an encouraable old

222
00:18:49,478 --> 00:18:55,158
hunks, and in his seagoing days, a bitter, hard taskmaster. They told me in

223
00:18:55,159 --> 00:18:59,718
Nantucket, though it certainly seems a curious story, that when he sailed the

224
00:18:59,719 --> 00:19:04,839
old Katagut whaleman, his crew, upon arriving home, were mostly all carried

225
00:19:04,840 --> 00:19:10,119
ashore to the hospital, sore, exhausted, and worn out. For a pious man,

226
00:19:10,120 --> 00:19:14,199
especially for a Quaker, he was certainly rather hard-hearted, to say

227
00:19:14,200 --> 00:19:18,999
the least. He never used to swear though at his men, they said, but somehow he

228
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:23,878
got an inordinate quantity of cruel, unmitigated hard work out of them. When

229
00:19:23,879 --> 00:19:28,759
Bildad was a chief mate, to have his drabcoled eye intently looking at you

230
00:19:28,760 --> 00:19:33,477
made you feel completely nervous, till you could clutch something, a hammer or

231
00:19:33,478 --> 00:19:38,679
a marlin spike, and go to work like mad at something or other, never mind what.

232
00:19:38,680 --> 00:19:43,237
Indolence and idleness perished before him. His own person was the exact

233
00:19:43,238 --> 00:19:46,438
embodiment of his utilitarian character.

234
00:19:46,439 --> 00:19:52,278
On his long gaunt body he carried no spare flesh, no superfluous beard, his

235
00:19:52,279 --> 00:19:59,317
chin having a soft economical nap to it, like the worn nap of his broadbrimmed hat.

236
00:19:59,318 --> 00:20:03,558
Such then was the person that I saw seated on the transom when I followed

237
00:20:03,559 --> 00:20:08,679
Captain Pelleg down into the cabin. The space between the decks was small, and

238
00:20:08,680 --> 00:20:14,199
there bolt upright sat old Billad, who always sat so, and never leaned, and

239
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:19,718
this to save his coattails. His broad brim was placed beside him. His legs

240
00:20:19,719 --> 00:20:24,358
were stiffly crossed, his drab vesture was buttoned up to his chin, and

241
00:20:24,359 --> 00:20:30,919
spectacles on nose he seemed absorbed in reading from a ponderous volume. "Bild!"

242
00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:35,718
cried Captain Pelleg. At it again, Billad, eh, you've been studying those

243
00:20:35,719 --> 00:20:42,759
scriptures now for the last 30 years, to my certain knowledge. How far you get, Bildad?

244
00:20:42,760 --> 00:20:47,798
As if long habituated to such profane talk from his old shipmate, Bildad,

245
00:20:47,799 --> 00:20:51,317
without noticing his present irreverence, quietly looked up, and

246
00:20:51,318 --> 00:20:56,759
seeing me, glanced again inquiringly towards Pelleg. He says he's our man,

247
00:20:56,760 --> 00:21:00,759
Bildad, said Pelleg. He wants to ship.

248
00:21:00,760 --> 00:21:07,237
Dust thee, said Bildad in a hollow tone, and turning round to me. I dust, said I,

249
00:21:07,238 --> 00:21:11,237
unconsciously. He was so intense a Quaker.

250
00:21:11,238 --> 00:21:14,999
What do you think of him, Billad? said Pelleg.

251
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:20,519
He'll do, said Billad, eyeing me, and then went on spelling away at his book

252
00:21:20,520 --> 00:21:23,878
in a mumbling tone quite audible.

253
00:21:23,879 --> 00:21:28,759
I thought him the queerest old Quaker I ever saw, especially as Pelleg, his

254
00:21:28,760 --> 00:21:33,397
friend and old shipmate, seemed such a bluster, but I said nothing, only

255
00:21:33,398 --> 00:21:37,958
looking round me sharply. Pelleg now threw open a chest, and drawing forth

256
00:21:37,959 --> 00:21:42,679
the ship's articles, placed pen and ink before him, and seated himself at a

257
00:21:42,680 --> 00:21:46,999
little table. I began to think it was high time to settle with myself at what

258
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:51,798
terms I would be willing to engage for the voyage. I was already aware that in

259
00:21:51,799 --> 00:21:55,798
the whailing business they paid no wages, but all hands, including the

260
00:21:55,799 --> 00:22:00,999
captain, received certain shares of the profit, called lays, and that these lays

261
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:04,919
were proportioned to the degree of importance pertaining to the respective

262
00:22:04,920 --> 00:22:09,638
duties of the ship's company. I was also aware that being a greenhand at

263
00:22:09,639 --> 00:22:14,119
whailing, my own lay could not be very large. But considering that I was used

264
00:22:14,120 --> 00:22:18,759
to the sea, could steer a ship, splice a rope, and all that, I made no doubt that

265
00:22:18,760 --> 00:22:23,718
from all I had heard, I should be offered at least the 275th lay, that is,

266
00:22:23,719 --> 00:22:28,999
the 275th part of the clear net proceeds of the voyage, whatever that might

267
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:34,679
eventually amount to. And though the 275th lay was what they call a rather

268
00:22:34,680 --> 00:22:39,558
long lay, yet it was better than nothing, and if we had a lucky voyage,

269
00:22:39,559 --> 00:22:43,638
might pretty nearly pay for the clothing I would wear out on it, not to speak of

270
00:22:43,639 --> 00:22:48,599
my 3 years beef and board for which I would not have to pay one styiver.

271
00:22:48,600 --> 00:22:52,599
It might be thought that this was a very poor way to accumulate a princely

272
00:22:52,600 --> 00:22:58,519
fortune, and so it was a very poor way indeed. But I am one of those that never

273
00:22:58,520 --> 00:23:02,839
take on about princely fortunes and am quite content if the world is ready to

274
00:23:02,840 --> 00:23:08,278
board and lodge me while I am putting up at this grim sign of the thundercloud.

275
00:23:08,279 --> 00:23:13,477
Upon the whole I thought that the 275th lay would be about the fair thing. But I

276
00:23:13,478 --> 00:23:17,718
would not have been surprised if I had been offered the 200th considering I was

277
00:23:17,719 --> 00:23:20,839
of a broad shouldered make.

278
00:23:20,840 --> 00:23:24,919
But one thing, nevertheless, that made me a little distrustful about receiving

279
00:23:24,920 --> 00:23:29,397
a generous share of the profits was this. Assure I had heard something of

280
00:23:29,398 --> 00:23:33,878
both Captain Pelleg and his unaccountable old crony Billad, how that

281
00:23:33,879 --> 00:23:38,519
they, being the principal proprietors of the Peekquad, therefore the other and

282
00:23:38,520 --> 00:23:42,759
more inconsiderable scattered owners left nearly the whole management of the

283
00:23:42,760 --> 00:23:48,039
ship's affairs to these two. And I did not know but what the stingy old Billad

284
00:23:48,040 --> 00:23:52,438
might have a mighty deal to say about shipping hands, especially as I now

285
00:23:52,439 --> 00:23:56,358
found him on board the Peekquad, quite ated home there in the cabin, and

286
00:23:56,359 --> 00:23:59,798
reading his Bible as if at his own fireside.

287
00:23:59,799 --> 00:24:04,679
Now, while Pelleg was vainly trying to mend a pen with his jack knife, old

288
00:24:04,680 --> 00:24:08,278
Bildad, to my no small surprise, considering that he was such an

289
00:24:08,279 --> 00:24:12,919
interested party in these proceedings, Bildad never heeded us, but went on

290
00:24:12,920 --> 00:24:17,798
mumbling to himself out of his book, "Lay not up for yourselves treasures

291
00:24:17,799 --> 00:24:22,999
upon earth, where m" Well, Captain Bildad, interrupted Pelleg. What do you

292
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:26,999
say? What lay shall we give this young man?

293
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:36,039
Thou knowest best, was the sephilcrol reply. The 777th wouldn't be too much, would it? Where

294
00:24:36,040 --> 00:24:45,397
moth and rust do corrupt. But lay lay indeed, thought I, and such a lay. The 777th.

295
00:24:45,398 --> 00:24:49,718
Well, old Bildad, you are determined that I, for one, shall not lay up many

296
00:24:49,719 --> 00:24:55,718
lays here below, where moth and rust do corrupt. It was an exceedingly long lay

297
00:24:55,719 --> 00:24:59,798
that indeed. And though from the magnitude of the figure it might at

298
00:24:59,799 --> 00:25:04,119
first deceive the landsmen, yet the slightest consideration will show that

299
00:25:04,120 --> 00:25:10,358
though 777 is a pretty large number, yet when you come to make a tenth of it, you

300
00:25:10,359 --> 00:25:14,119
will see as I say that the 777th part of

301
00:25:14,120 --> 00:25:20,759
a farthing is a good deal less than 777 gold dloons. And so thought I at the

302
00:25:20,760 --> 00:25:26,519
time. I blast your eyes, Billad, cried Pelleg. Thou does not want to swindle

303
00:25:26,520 --> 00:25:30,039
this young man. He must have more than that.

304
00:25:30,040 --> 00:25:37,158
777th again, said Billad without lifting his eyes, and then went on mumbling. For

305
00:25:37,159 --> 00:25:42,599
where your treasure is, there will your heart be also. I am going to put him

306
00:25:42,600 --> 00:25:49,958
down for the 300th, said Pelleg. Do you hear that, Bildad? The 300th lay, I say.

307
00:25:49,959 --> 00:25:55,718
Bildad laid down his book, and turning solemnly towards him, said, "Captain

308
00:25:55,719 --> 00:26:01,237
Pelleg, thou hast a generous heart, but thou must consider the duty thou owe us

309
00:26:01,238 --> 00:26:06,438
to the other owners of this ship, widows and orphans, many of them, and that if

310
00:26:06,439 --> 00:26:11,317
we too abundantly reward the labors of this young man, we may be taking bread

311
00:26:11,318 --> 00:26:14,039
from those widows and those orphans."

312
00:26:14,040 --> 00:26:21,798
The 777th lay, Captain Pelleg. Thou, Bildad, roared Pelleg, starting up and

313
00:26:21,799 --> 00:26:26,358
clattering about the cabin. Last year, Captain Bildad, if I had followed thy

314
00:26:26,359 --> 00:26:30,759
advice in these matters, I would have for now had a conscience to lug about,

315
00:26:30,760 --> 00:26:37,237
and would be heavy enough to founder the largest ship that ever sailed round Cape Horn.

316
00:26:37,238 --> 00:26:43,317
Captain Pelleg said Bildad steadily. Thy conscience may be drawing 10 in of water

317
00:26:43,318 --> 00:26:49,158
or 10 fathoms, I can't tell. But as thou art still an impenitent man, Captain

318
00:26:49,159 --> 00:26:54,599
Pelleg, I greatly fear, lest thy conscience be but a leaky one, and will

319
00:26:54,600 --> 00:27:00,519
in the end sink thee foundering down to the fiery pit, Captain Pelleg. Fiery

320
00:27:00,520 --> 00:27:07,477
pit, fiery pit. You insult me, man. past all natural bearing. You insult me. It's

321
00:27:07,478 --> 00:27:12,519
an allfired outrage to tell any human creature that he's bound to hell. Fluks

322
00:27:12,520 --> 00:27:18,839
and flames. Bildad. Say that again to me and start my soul bolts. I'll I'll Yes,

323
00:27:18,840 --> 00:27:24,119
I'll swallow a live goat with all his hair and horns on. Out of the cabin, ye

324
00:27:24,120 --> 00:27:30,278
caning drabcoled son of a wooden gun. A straight wake with ye.

325
00:27:30,279 --> 00:27:35,237
As he thundered out this, he made a rush at Bildad, but with a marvelous oblique

326
00:27:35,238 --> 00:27:40,919
sliding solarity, Bildad for that time eluded him. Alarmed at this terrible

327
00:27:40,920 --> 00:27:45,158
outburst between the two principal and responsible owners of the ship, and

328
00:27:45,159 --> 00:27:49,477
feeling half a mind to give up all idea of sailing in a vessel so questionably

329
00:27:49,478 --> 00:27:54,438
owned, and temporarily commanded, I stepped aside from the door to give

330
00:27:54,439 --> 00:27:59,158
egress to Bildad, who I made no doubt was all eagerness to vanish before the

331
00:27:59,159 --> 00:28:04,358
awakened wrath of Pelleg. But to my astonishment he sat down again on the

332
00:28:04,359 --> 00:28:08,358
transom very quietly and seemed to have not the slightest intention of

333
00:28:08,359 --> 00:28:14,039
withdrawing. He seemed quite used to the impenitent Pelleg in his ways. As for

334
00:28:14,040 --> 00:28:19,237
Pelleg, after letting off his rage as he had, there seemed no more left in him,

335
00:28:19,238 --> 00:28:26,999
and he too sat down like a lamb, though he twitched a little as if still nervously agitated.

336
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:31,718
He whistled at last. A squall's gone off to lure it, I think.

337
00:28:31,719 --> 00:28:36,278
Bildad, thou used to be good at sharpening a lance. Mend that pen, will

338
00:28:36,279 --> 00:28:40,438
you? My jack knife here needs the grindstoneone. That's he. Thank you,

339
00:28:40,439 --> 00:28:46,039
Bildad. Now then, my young man, Ishmael is thy name, didn't you say? Well, then

340
00:28:46,040 --> 00:28:51,638
down you go here, Ishmail, for the 300th lay. Captain Pelleg, said I. I have a

341
00:28:51,639 --> 00:28:53,638
friend with me who wants a ship, too.

342
00:28:53,639 --> 00:28:58,519
Shall I bring him down tomorrow? To be sure, said Pelleg. Fetch him along, and

343
00:28:58,520 --> 00:29:04,119
we'll look at him. What lay does he want? groaned Bildad, glancing up from

344
00:29:04,120 --> 00:29:08,039
the book in which he had again been burying himself.

345
00:29:08,040 --> 00:29:13,078
Ah, never mind about that, Billad said Pelleg. Has he ever wailed at any

346
00:29:13,079 --> 00:29:18,278
turning to me killed more whales than I can count, Captain Pelleg. Well, bring

347
00:29:18,279 --> 00:29:23,878
him along then. And after signing the papers, off I went, nothing doubting but

348
00:29:23,879 --> 00:29:27,558
that I had done a good morning's work, and that the Peekquad was the identical

349
00:29:27,559 --> 00:29:32,679
ship that Yojo had provided to carry Quiquake and me round the Cape. But I

350
00:29:32,680 --> 00:29:36,358
had not proceeded far when I began to be think me that the captain with whom I

351
00:29:36,359 --> 00:29:39,798
was to sail yet remained unseen by me.

352
00:29:39,799 --> 00:29:44,278
Though indeed in many cases a whale ship will be completely fitted out and

353
00:29:44,279 --> 00:29:48,438
receive all her crew on board the captain makes himself visible by

354
00:29:48,439 --> 00:29:53,237
arriving to take command. For sometimes these voyages are so prolonged, and the

355
00:29:53,238 --> 00:29:57,798
shore intervals at home so exceedingly brief, that if the captain have a

356
00:29:57,799 --> 00:30:02,519
family, or any absorbing concernment of that sort, he does not trouble himself

357
00:30:02,520 --> 00:30:06,679
much about his ship in port, but leaves her to the owners till all is ready for

358
00:30:06,680 --> 00:30:11,798
sea. However, it is always as well to have a look at him before irrevocably

359
00:30:11,799 --> 00:30:14,039
committing yourself into his hands.

360
00:30:14,040 --> 00:30:18,759
Turning back, I accosted Captain Pelleg, inquiring where Captain Ahab was to be

361
00:30:18,760 --> 00:30:23,317
found. And what does thou want of Captain Ahab? It's all right enough.

362
00:30:23,318 --> 00:30:28,519
Thou art shipped. Yes, but I should like to see him. But I don't think you wilt

363
00:30:28,520 --> 00:30:32,278
be able to at present. I don't know exactly what's the matter with him, but

364
00:30:32,279 --> 00:30:37,878
he keeps close inside the house. A sort of sick, and yet he don't look so. In

365
00:30:37,879 --> 00:30:43,798
fact, he ain't sick. But no, he isn't well either. Anyhow, young man, he won't

366
00:30:43,799 --> 00:30:48,438
always see me, so I don't suppose he will thee. He's a queer man, Captain

367
00:30:48,439 --> 00:30:53,638
Ahab. So some think, but a good one. Oh, thou like him well enough. No fear, no

368
00:30:53,639 --> 00:30:59,078
fear. He's a grand, ungodly, godlike man, Captain Ahab. Doesn't speak much,

369
00:30:59,079 --> 00:31:04,919
but when he does speak, then you may well listen. Mark, be forewarned. Ahab's

370
00:31:04,920 --> 00:31:08,919
above the common. Ahab's been in colleges, as well as amongst the

371
00:31:08,920 --> 00:31:14,199
cannibals. been used to deeper wonders than the waves. Fixed his fiery lance in

372
00:31:14,200 --> 00:31:20,039
mightier stranger foes than whales. His lance I the keenest and the shest that

373
00:31:20,040 --> 00:31:24,999
out of all our aisle. Oh, he ain't Captain Bildad. No, and he ain't Captain

374
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:31,798
Pelleg. He's Ahab, boy. And Ahab of old thou knowest was a crowned king and a

375
00:31:31,799 --> 00:31:38,438
very vile one. When that wicked king was slain, the dogs did they not lick his blood?

376
00:31:38,439 --> 00:31:43,158
Come hither to me. Hither. Hither, said Pelleg, with a significance in his eye

377
00:31:43,159 --> 00:31:46,599
that almost startled me. Look ye lad.

378
00:31:46,600 --> 00:31:48,919
Never say that on board the Peekquad.

379
00:31:48,920 --> 00:31:54,199
Never say it anywhere. Captain Ahab did not name himself. It was a foolish,

380
00:31:54,200 --> 00:31:58,759
ignorant whim of his crazy widowed mother who died when he was only 12

381
00:31:58,760 --> 00:32:03,878
month old. And yet the old squa Tistig at Gayhead said that the name would

382
00:32:03,879 --> 00:32:08,999
somehow prove prophetic. and perhaps other fools like her may tell thee the

383
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:15,477
same. I wish to warn thee, it's a lie. I know Captain Ahab well. I've sailed with

384
00:32:15,478 --> 00:32:21,638
him as mate years ago. I know what he is. A good man. Not a pious good man

385
00:32:21,639 --> 00:32:26,999
like Bildad, but a swearing good man, something like me. Only there's a good

386
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:30,839
deal more of him. I I I know that he was

387
00:32:30,840 --> 00:32:35,477
never very jolly, and I know that on the passage home he was a little out of his

388
00:32:35,478 --> 00:32:40,199
mind for a spell, but it was the sharp shooting pains in his bleeding stump

389
00:32:40,200 --> 00:32:44,679
that brought that about, as anyone might see. I know, too, that ever since he

390
00:32:44,680 --> 00:32:50,199
lost his leg last voyage by that oursed whale, he's been a kind of moody,

391
00:32:50,200 --> 00:32:53,158
desperate, moody, and savage sometimes.

392
00:32:53,159 --> 00:32:57,477
But that will all pass off. And once for all let me tell thee and assure thee

393
00:32:57,478 --> 00:33:02,039
young man, it's better to sail with a moody good captain than a laughing bad

394
00:33:02,040 --> 00:33:07,078
one. So goodbye to thee, and wrong not Captain Ahab, because he happens to have

395
00:33:07,079 --> 00:33:13,237
a wicked name. Besides, my boy, he has a wife not three voyages wedded, a sweet

396
00:33:13,238 --> 00:33:18,278
resigned girl. Think of that. And by that sweet girl, that old man has a

397
00:33:18,279 --> 00:33:24,519
child. Hold ye then, can there be any utter hopeless harm in Ahab? No. No, my

398
00:33:24,520 --> 00:33:32,839
lad. Stricken, blasted if he be, Ahab has his humanities.

399
00:33:32,840 --> 00:33:36,679
As I walked away, I was full of thoughtfulness. What had been

400
00:33:36,680 --> 00:33:41,078
incidentally revealed to me of Captain Ahab filled me with a certain wild

401
00:33:41,079 --> 00:33:45,718
vagueness of painfulness concerning him, and somehow at the time I felt a

402
00:33:45,719 --> 00:33:50,679
sympathy and a sorrow for him, but for I don't know what, unless it was the cruel

403
00:33:50,680 --> 00:33:52,919
loss of his leg.

404
00:33:52,920 --> 00:33:57,878
And yet I also felt a strange awe of him, but that sort of awe which I cannot

405
00:33:57,879 --> 00:34:04,358
at all describe was not exactly awe. I do not know what it was, but I felt it,

406
00:34:04,359 --> 00:34:08,839
and it did not disincin me towards him, though I felt impatience at what seemed

407
00:34:08,840 --> 00:34:14,278
like mystery in him so imperfectly as he was known to me then. However, my

408
00:34:14,279 --> 00:34:18,759
thoughts were at length carried in other directions, so that for the present dark

409
00:34:18,760 --> 00:34:23,040
Ahab slipped my mind.
